Below is a complete set of **FTP client‑request templates** written in the same “list‑of‑strings” style you used for the RTSP DESCRIBE and HTTP GET examples.  
Each template is a one‑line command (terminated with `\r\n`) that you can populate by replacing the `<<…>>` placeholders with the actual values you need.

```python
FTP_CLIENT_REQUEST_TEMPLATES = {
    # --- Authentication ---
    "USER": ["USER <<USERNAME>>\r\n"],
    "PASS": ["PASS <<PASSWORD>>\r\n"],
    "ACCT": ["ACCT <<ACCOUNT>>\r\n"],

    # --- File‑system navigation ---
    "CWD":  ["CWD <<PATH>>\r\n"],
    "CDUP": ["CDUP\r\n"],
    "PWD":  ["PWD\r\n"],

    # --- Directory operations ---
    "MKD":  ["MKD <<DIRNAME>>\r\n"],
    "RMD":  ["RMD <<DIRNAME>>\r\n"],
    "LIST": ["LIST <<PATH>>\r\n"],
    "NLST": ["NLST <<PATH>>\r\n"],

    # --- File transfer ---
    "RETR": ["RETR <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],
    "STOR": ["STOR <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],
    "APPE": ["APPE <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],
    "ALLO": ["ALLO <<SIZE>>\r\n"],
    "REST": ["REST <<OFFSET>>\r\n"],

    # --- File deletion & rename ---
    "DELE": ["DELE <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],
    "RNFR": ["RNFR <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],
    "RNTO": ["RNTO <<FILENAME>>\r\n"],

    # --- Connection mode ---
    "PASV": ["PASV\r\n"],
    "PORT": ["PORT <<IP>>,<<PORT>>\r\n"],

    # --- Transfer parameters ---
    "TYPE": ["TYPE <<TYPE>>\r\n"],          # e.g. A, I, E, L
    "MODE": ["MODE <<MODE>>\r\n"],          # e.g. S, B, C
    "STRU": ["STRU <<STRUCTURE>>\r\n"],    # e.g. F, R, P

    # --- Miscellaneous ---
    "QUIT": ["QUIT\r\n"],
    "NOOP": ["NOOP\r\n"],
    "SYST": ["SYST\r\n"],
    "STAT": ["STAT <<PATH>>\r\n"],
    "HELP": ["HELP <<COMMAND>>\r\n"],
    "SITE": ["SITE <<STRING>>\r\n"],

    # --- Extended commands (RFC 2389, RFC 4217, etc.) ---
    "FEAT": ["FEAT\r\n"],
    "OPTS": ["OPTS <<OPTIONS>>\r\n"],
    "PBSZ": ["PBSZ <<SIZE>>\r\n"],
    "PROT": ["PROT <<LEVEL>>\r\n"],        # e.g. C, P
}
```

### How to use it
1. **Select the command** you need (e.g., `"RETR"`).  
2. **Fill in the placeholders** – e.g., `<<FILENAME>>` → `"myfile.txt"`.  
3. **Send the resulting string** over the control‑connection (port 21 or the TLS‑wrapped port).  

The templates follow the FTP specification (RFC 959) and the common extensions (RFC 2389, RFC 4217, etc.). Feel free to add or remove entries as your application requires. Happy coding!
